        "mutation_id": "a49e7b72-43e6-40e9-9274-ce4eaf2ec774",
        "proposal": {
          "diagnosis": "The system suffers from extreme passenger delays (76,335 minutes), peak station queue buildup (up to 712 passengers at Townsend and 549 at SF Caltrain), and 1,218 unserved passengers during major venue egress events (Chase Center and Oracle Park). While Muni repeatedly dispatches standby vehicles reactively once local queues breach corridor capacities, Caltrain and BART cannot increase fleet capacity and receive no forewarning of approaching transfer waves. Consequently, inter-operator transfer nodes experience severe queue spikes and missed connections because regional dispatch decisions are reactive rather than anticipatory.",
          "rationale": "To resolve inter-agency queue cascades without violating fleet constraints (3 Muni standby pairs, fixed BART/Caltrain fleets), the harness requires anticipatory multi-modal planning. Integrating demand_forecaster with the demand_forecast tool alongside regional_coordinator enables the regional_predictive optimization strategy under regional_shared context. Setting memory_window_steps to 12 across 5-minute replanning intervals allows agents to capture the full 60-minute progression of surge waves, enabling the coordinator to meter Muni dispatches and align transfer windows before physical station bottlenecks form. Strict verification guarantees that proposed standby schedules strictly respect vehicle and driver availability limits.",
          "expected_improvement": "Hypothesizes that predictive event-surge forecasting and regional multi-agent coordination will meter passenger flows into Caltrain and BART hubs earlier, flattening peak station queues and lowering total delay and unserved passenger counts compared to reactive dispatching.",
